 "observations": [
  "The page number «154» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 43», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. The titles (L01-L03), in bold, the sigla in roman; L03, «Locus autem draconis 0S. 21 G. 54 M.», without the «eius» of pp. 151-153, in smaller bold type, the S set close to the 0, followed by «(CN)». The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of L03 and below it, «C 64r, O 121v.» and «N 17v.», the letters raised; here as left margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on p. 151 (JP2 0183), with «Pagina numerorum (O) Semita numeri (C)» over the four columns of the arguments, divided below a rule into «Augmentum proprietatum omnium1)» and «Diminutio proprietatum omnium1)» in smaller type; «Sublimatio ascensus Martis examinata2)»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza3)» and «Examinatio centri», each hyphenated «Exami-natio»; «Statio prima Martis»; «Latitudo Martis descendentis4)» over «prima» and «secunda» in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L04-L08), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L09, the units, in bold.",
  "L10-L39. Checked as on p. 150 (JP2 0182): the Sublimatio is 4s 8° 24′ less half the Examinatio argumenti in every row, a half minute counted as a whole; it reaches its least value, 3s 18° 9′, at 127°-130°, where the Examinatio argumenti has its greatest, 40° 31′ at 128° and 129°. The radius that reproduces each value of the Examinatio argumenti as the equation of an epicycle is between 38.85 and 39.09 in every row; an epicycle of radius 39 has its greatest equation, arc sin (39 : 60) = 40° 32.5′, at 130.5°.",
  "L10-L39. The Latitudo secunda is 3° 45′ · sin x to within 0.6″ in every row except at 126°, 133° and 148°, and its value for 180° − y is that of y on p. 151 (JP2 0183) except there. At 126° (L15), 3° 2′ 3″ is 1.3″ more than 3° 2′ 1.7″ (p. 151 at 54°: 3° 2′ 2″). At 133° (L22), 2° 44′ 13″ is 20″ less than 3° 45′ · sin 133° = 2° 44′ 33.3″, the 2° 44′ 33″ of p. 151 at 47°; no note of the apparatus bears on the 13. At 148° (L37) the edition prints 1° 59′ 15″ (note k) against the 55 of C and O, 1.1″ more than 1° 59′ 13.9″, where p. 151 has 1° 59′ 14″ at 32°; the note does not say «correxi».",
  "L10-L39, the other columns: the Examinatio centri for 180° − y is that of y on pp. 150-151 (JP2 0182-0183) in every row except at 146° (L35), 6° 13′ against the 6° 12′ of 34°; it decreases by 7′ a degree to 135° and then by 9′ or 10′, to 5° 35′ at 150°, the value of 30°. The Statio prima increases from 5s 16° 16′ to 5s 18° 21′: by 5′ a degree to 133°, by 4′ (once 5′) to 144° and by 3′ or 4′ from 144° on. The Latitudo prima decreases from 1° 9′ at 121° by 1′ a degree, with equal values at 124°-125°, 132°-133°, 142°-143° and 148°-149°, to 0° 44′ at 150°.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note a: the last argument, 5s 0°, is C's; O has 4. Note 4: the head «Latitudo Martis descendentis» is O's; C and N have «recessus Martis». Checked: the Sublimatio 3s 19° 57′ at 149° (note b, C, against O's 20) agrees with the rule; the degrees 7 of the Examinatio centri at 135° and 139° (notes c, O, against C's 8) and its minutes 27, 17 and 53 at 122°, 132° and 148° (notes d, e, f) are the values of 45°, 41°, 58°, 48° and 32° on pp. 150-151; the Latitudo prima 0° 50′ at 142° (note g, C, against O's 1) agrees with its column; the Latitudo secunda 2° 33′ 27″, 2° 18′ 31″ and 1° 52′ 30″ at 137°, 142° and 150° (notes h, i, l), 2° 33′ 27.0″, 2° 18′ 31.4″ and 1° 52′ 30.0″ computed.",
  "Italics: «om.». N01: the ellipses «. . . .» of the lemmata as printed. The folio line «C 64r, O 121v.» with a point (before L03) and N01, «Sublimafio»: see the critical notes."
 ],
